## Keep it lean (after Ponytail, github.com/DietrichGebert/ponytail)

The owner wants the project to stay simple as it grows. Before writing new code, go down this
ladder and stop at the first step that works:
1. Is it needed at all? (Don't build for a "maybe later".)
2. Does the codebase already have it? (Search `src/lib`, `src/components/ui`, `src/server` first.)
3. Does the language, the browser or the platform do it (Web APIs, CSS, Next.js, Workers, D1)?
4. Does a package already installed do it? Don't add a new package for something small.
5. Can it be one line or a few? Otherwise the smallest version that works, plus one small test for
   any logic.

Never "simplify" away input checks (`src/server/body.ts`), error handling that protects data, sign-in
and access checks, security headers or accessibility (labels, contrast, keyboard, Reduce Motion).

- Delete instead of commenting out. When something replaces old code, remove the old code in the
  same change.
- A known shortcut gets a `shortcut:` comment saying what was left out and when it would matter.
- In the summary to the owner, say what was left out on purpose.
- `pnpm lean` (also part of `pnpm check`) lists files nothing imports and links to pages that don't
  exist. Keep it at zero. A file used only from config goes in its `BY_CONFIG` list, with the reason.
- The Ponytail plugin itself installs only from Claude Code, typed by the owner:
  `/plugin marketplace add DietrichGebert/ponytail`. These rules cover what it does, so it's optional.

## Design and motion (after Impeccable and Emil Kowalski's skills)

Adapted, in our own words, from github.com/pbakaus/impeccable (Apache-2.0) and
github.com/emilkowalski/skills (MIT). The owner's look comes first: the two tones (dark blue #253745 into
dark pink #6B5560, the owner's palettes since 23 Oct 2026), the glow, the light river and the italic
accent word are the brand, even where those guides call them "AI-looking". Never swap them for
another palette.

Motion (Emil Kowalski):
- Ask first: how often is it seen? Something used many times a day (keyboard shortcuts, the command
  palette, list navigation) gets no animation or almost none. Every animation needs a reason:
  where something came from, a change of state, feedback, or explaining how something works.
- Entering: ease-out, never ease-in (it feels slow). Use the project's curves (`--ease-glide`,
  `--ease-ios`, `--ease-out-soft` in globals.css, `spring` in `@/lib/motion`), not plain `ease`.
- UI motion stays under 300 ms (press 100–160, menus 150–250, sheets up to 500); exits faster than
  entrances; explanatory motion (the landing page, flow cards) may be longer.
- Nothing grows from nothing: start at scale 0.85–0.95 with opacity 0, never `scale: 0`. Menus and
  popovers grow from where they were opened (`origin-top-right`, `rtl:origin-top-left`).
- Name the properties: `transition-[transform,opacity]` or Tailwind's `transition`, not
  `transition-all` in new code (it animates layout and costs frames). Animate transform and opacity;
  avoid animating width, height, top or left.
- Lists that appear together are staggered 30–80 ms. Reduce Motion always stops movement.
- Phones: hover-only extras sit behind `@media (hover: hover)` (Tailwind's `hover:` does this);
  something that only shows on hover must show without it on touch screens.

Look and reading (Impeccable):
- Text that carries meaning is 11 px or more (12 px for running text). No new `text-[10px]`.
- Running text stays near 75 characters a line (`max-w-[75ch]`); headings balance, paragraphs don't
  leave one word alone (globals.css sets `text-wrap: balance` and `pretty`).
- Grey text needs 4.5:1 against what's really behind it (a see-through card on a light page turns
  grey: check both themes). Never grey text on a coloured background without checking.
- No cards inside cards; spacing and type sizes from the scale already in use.
- To find problems on a page, Impeccable's checker can scan it: `npx impeccable detect <url>` (it
  downloads the checker the first time). Judge its findings against the brand rule above.

## Optional: Everything Claude Code (ECC)

The owner may want **Everything Claude Code** (github.com/affaan-m/everything-claude-code), a large
add-on pack for Claude Code (and partly Cursor): agents, skills, slash commands, rules, hooks.
Rules for using it here:
- **Don't install the whole pack**: its catalogue is sent with every request (slower, costlier).
  Install a minimal profile or pick single pieces (e.g. a planning or review skill).
- **Read every hook before turning it on**: hooks run commands automatically. Never enable anything
  that sends code or data to another service (some commands send diffs to other AI providers).
- It never overrides this file: the owner's rules, safety rules and the plan come first.
- Ask the owner before installing it, and say what you're adding and why.
- **Installed (20 Oct 2026, at the owner's request), fitted to this project:** in `.claude/` the
  commands `/plan-item`, `/check`, `/fix-checks`, `/finish-item`, `/council`; the reviewers
  `universe-reviewer` and `universe-security` (only when asked; no model pinned); and one hook,
  `protect-secrets.mjs` (blocks reading or writing the local secret files). See the root `CLAUDE.md`.
- **Left out on purpose:** ECC's hooks (one blocks `pnpm dev`, one blocks new `.md` notes, others
  run Prettier or a full type check after every edit, which takes minutes here), its "use
  proactively / must be used" agents (they start an extra AI session after every change), its MCP
  servers (they need keys and send data to other services), the memory and learning hooks, and its
  generic skills (written for other stacks: Supabase, Redis, older Next.js).
- **22 Oct 2026: the worldflowai copy (github.com/worldflowai/everything-claude-code) checked** at the
  owner's request. It's the same pack (agents, commands, the same hooks), set up for that company's
  own projects: nothing in it beats what's installed above (its `/verify` and "verification loop" are
  `/check`; its build fixer is `/fix-checks`; its planner and reviewers are `/plan-item` and the two
  reviewers). One piece is worth having: the "strategic compact" reminder (counts tool calls and,
  after about 60, suggests `/compact` or `/clear` once an item is finished, so long histories aren't
  re-sent). It runs only on this computer, but changing `.claude/settings.json` needs the owner's
  own approval in Claude Code, so it's not turned on yet.
- **22 Oct 2026: the current pack (github.com/affaan-m/ecc, 311 skills) checked for smoothness.**
  Its motion skills (`motion-foundations`, `motion-patterns`, `motion-advanced`) describe what the
  M1 motion layer already does (word reveals, count-ups, path drawing, cursor light, pausing when
  off screen), so they aren't installed. Some of their rules clash with this project ("never
  `framer-motion`": we use `m` from it; page transitions that wait for the old page: ours never
  wait, see `page-transition.ts`). Taken from them: decorative blur and drifting light are left out
  on low-memory phones (`lowEndDevice`). `liquid-glass-design` is for SwiftUI apps, not the web.

## Gotchas already learned

- React hooks lint (v6): no reading refs while rendering, no `setState` directly in an effect body
  (in a callback or a promise is fine), no reassigning variables after render, no `Date.now()` while
  rendering (`src/lib/use-now.ts`).
- axe "label-content-name-mismatch": don't give a button an `aria-label` different from its visible
  text; add `sr-only` text instead. An absolutely positioned `sr-only` span inside an
  `overflow-x-auto` toolbar can widen the page: make the toolbar `relative`.
- ProseMirror reads a keyboard selection a moment after the key: pause ~150 ms in browser tests
  before a toolbar action.
- Attendance rows are stored at midnight UTC of the class day. D1 supports FTS5 and window functions.
- KaTeX: import `katex/dist/katex.mjs` (plain `katex` may be the CommonJS copy that the chemistry
  extension doesn't extend).
- In browser tests, mark the welcome tour as seen before loading a page (`localStorage` key
  `universe-tour-done:<user id>`), or it covers the page and blocks clicks.
- In browser tests, measure elements only after the calendar's days finish sliding in; phone tab
  bars can cover elements near the bottom (scroll them into the middle first).
